<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Shah of Pahang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1470–<a href="/years/1475">1475</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Shah was the first sultan of Pahang on the east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-shah-of-malacca">Sultan Mansur Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, he was installed by his father around 1470 after Pahang was taken from Siamese influence, and held court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekan">Pekan</a>. His line ruled Pahang as a branch of the Malacca royal house until the 17th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Known before his accession as Raja Muhammad, he was the second of two sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-shah-of-malacca">Sultan Mansur Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> by Putri Wanang Sri Lela Wangsa, daughter of Dewa Sura, the last pre-Malaccan ruler of Pahang, who was related to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Ligor</a>; mother and grandfather had been captured and brought to Malacca after the conquest of Pahang in 1454. His father favoured him over his elder brother, Raja Ahmad, and named him Raja Muda, heir apparent, but the boy had a reputation for a hot temper.</p><p>When he was about fifteen he rode past boys playing sepak raga, and a ball kicked by Tun Besar, son of the Bendahara Tun Perak, knocked off his headdress. In a rage he drew his kris and killed Tun Besar. The Bendahara restrained his people from taking revenge, reminding them that Malay folks never commit treason, but they vowed that Raja Muhammad should never rule over them. Sultan Mansur agreed to send him away and ordered Sri Bija Diraja to escort him to Pahang and install him there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He brought the trappings of the Malaccan court with him: Tun Hamzah, the former governor of Pahang, as his first Bendahara, Seri Akar Raja as chief Hulubalang, a chief treasurer, a Temenggong, and 100 boys and 100 girls of noble family. Installed as Sultan Muhammad Shah in 1470, he modelled his government on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>'s, following the Undang-Undang Melaka and the Undang-Undang Laut Melaka. Pahang remained a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Malacca. His realm stretched from Sedili Besar to the borders of Terengganu, and his court is said to have stood at Tanjung Langgar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekan">Pekan</a>. He married Mengindera Puteri, a granddaughter of Sultan Iskandar Shah of Kelantan, and had three sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 17 September 1475, possibly of poison according to Portuguese records, and was buried at Langgar, Dusun Pinang, which gave him his posthumous name, Marhum Langgar. His son Raja Jamil succeeded him. The Arabic epitaph on his tombstone records his descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-shah-of-malacca">Sultan Mansur Shah</a>, Muzaffar Shah and Muhammad Shah and the date of his death, and confirms the genealogy of the Malaccan sultans given in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Shah of Pahang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1470 Sultan Mansur Shah of Malacca installed his son <b>Muhammad Shah</b> as its first sultan, with his court at Pekan near the mouth of the Pahang River.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-pahang-sultanate">Old Pahang Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Malacca sultanate. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=0.54,101.45&amp;year=1472" aria-label="Riau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Muhammad Shah of Pahang"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5973 4954h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5973 4954h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=0.54,101.45&amp;year=1472">What was here in 1472? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
